+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## The problem
+
+When a player with a 20,000-block saved build joined, the server froze for about 2.1 s in one
+frame (MicroProfiler, almost all of it under `Script_Builds`) and every player rubber-banded.
+Clients then hitched in `Replicator ProcessPackets`. Leaving caused a smaller freeze.
+
+Causes, in `src/ServerScriptService/Builds.server.luau` before this change:
+
+1. **All of it ran in one frame.** `loadBuild` created every block in a single loop, and
+   `onPlayerRemoving` destroyed the whole plot folder at once. The server could not step physics
+   or send network updates until the loop finished. Then the whole build reached every client in
+   one burst, which is the `ProcessPackets` hitch.
+2. **Three instances per block.** Each block was a `Part` plus an `Owner` `StringValue` and a
+   `PlacedAt` `NumberValue`. A 20,000-block build was 60,000 instances to create, replicate,
+   keep in memory (about 600 bytes per value object), and destroy.
+3. **Parent set first.** `Instance.new("Part", parent)` parents the part before its properties
+   are set, so every later property write also goes through replication. Measured on a
+   baseplate, that costs about 1.6x as much as setting the properties first and parenting last.
+
+## What changed
+
+- **Loading and unloading run across frames.** One scheduler on `Heartbeat` creates or destroys
+  blocks until 3 ms of server time have been used that frame (`FRAME_ALLOWANCE`), then continues
+  on the next frame. The 3 ms is shared by every plot, so eight players joining at once cost no
+  more per frame than one. Plots are served first come, first served. The work shows up in the
+  MicroProfiler under the `Builds:Step` label.
+- **One instance per block.** The owner and the placed time are now attributes on the part:
+  `OwnerId` (number, the owner's UserId) and `PlacedAt` (number, Unix time, same value as
+  before). Clients can still read both, which the upcoming placed-time feature can use. The
+  server keeps its own block-to-plot table for the owner check on removal, so the check never
+  reads anything from the instance.
+- **Properties first, `Parent` last** when a block is created.
+- **Saved cells are reserved when loading starts.** The build's cells are marked occupied in the
+  first frame (a pure Luau loop with no instances), so no one can place a block into a cell the
+  build is about to fill.
+- **Unloading keeps the plot's slot until its last block is gone.** The departing player's
+  blocks are destroyed over several frames, and each cell is freed as its block is destroyed.
+  Blocks that were never created (the player left mid-load) have their reserved cells freed
+  right away.
+- **Players who join while every slot is busy wait for one.** Before, a slot was freed on the
+  same frame its owner left. Now a slot can still be unloading, so a player who joins a full
+  server right then waits in a queue and gets the slot when the unload finishes.
+- `SavedBuilds.load` may now yield (the DataStore version will). If the player leaves during
+  the load, the load stops and nothing is created.
+
+Placing and removing are unchanged: same remotes, same validation, same reach and rate limits,
+and removal still only works on your own blocks.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Builds appear over about two seconds instead of all at once.** A 20,000-block build fills
+  in block by block, in saved order (bottom layer first with the current test build). The owner
+  can place and remove blocks while it loads.
+- **Builds disappear over about two seconds after their owner leaves.** No one can remove those
+  blocks or place into their cells until each one is gone.
+- **When several big builds load at once, they load one after another**, so the last one can
+  take several times as long (about 2 s per 20,000 blocks, estimated).
+- **On a full server, a player who joins just after someone with a big build leaves** can wait
+  up to about two seconds for their plot to start loading.
+- **No server-wide freeze or rubber-banding** when big builds load or unload, and no single
+  large `ProcessPackets` hitch on clients. The same blocks still replicate, but at about
+  3 ms of server work per frame.
+
+## Expected numbers (not yet measured)
+
+These are estimates from baseplate measurements of instance creation, not measurements in this
+place. Check them before calling this done.
+
+| Metric | Before | After (expected) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Worst server frame while a 20,000-block build loads | about 2,100 ms | about 3 ms in `Builds:Step`, plus normal frame cost |
+| Instances per block | 3 | 1 |
+| Time until a 20,000-block build is fully visible | one frame (frozen) | about 1.5 to 2.5 s at 60 Hz |
+| Worst server frame while it unloads | the smaller freeze from your report | about 3 ms in `Builds:Step` |
+
+## How to verify
+
+1. In Studio, start a local server with 2 or more players (Test, Clients and Servers).
+2. Open the server's MicroProfiler (Ctrl+F6 on the server view, or the Developer Console), and
+   have one player join and leave while you record.
+3. Check that no frame spikes above about 20 ms during the load and the unload, that
+   `Builds:Step` stays near 3 ms per frame, and how many frames the load spans.
+4. On a client, record during another player's join and check `ProcessPackets` for spikes.
+5. Check by hand: you can place and remove your own blocks while your build loads; you cannot
+   remove another player's blocks; leaving mid-load leaves no stray blocks behind; after a
+   departing build finishes unloading, its cells can be built on again; and `PlacedAt` and
+   `OwnerId` show in the Properties panel on a selected block.
+
+If loading takes too long, raise `FRAME_ALLOWANCE`. If server frames are still heavy, lower it.
+
+## Not changed
+
+- `SavedBuilds.load` still builds the 20,000-entry test table in one call, and reserving cells
+  walks it in one frame. Both are plain Luau work (expected a few ms) and were left alone until
+  a profile shows them.
+- Every block is still its own replicated `Part`. If joins or memory become the next problem,
+  the next step is streaming (`Workspace.StreamingEnabled`) or keeping builds as data on the
+  server and having clients build the visuals.
